Can someone attend all their meetings? Sort by start and check each meeting against the one right after it:

```python
meetings.sort()
ok = all(meetings[i][1] <= meetings[i + 1][0] for i in range(len(meetings) - 1))
```

Trace for `[(9, 10), (13, 15), (10, 12)]`:

| sorted | end of this | start of next | clash? |
|---|---|---|---|
| (9, 10) | 10 | 10 | no — touching is fine |
| (10, 12) | 12 | 13 | no |
| (13, 15) | — | — | — |

The same check in C++:

```cpp
sort(m.begin(), m.end());
bool ok = true;
for (int i = 0; i + 1 < n; i++)
    if (m[i].second > m[i + 1].first) ok = false;
```

In Java, sort an `int[][]` with a comparator. Use `Integer.compare(a[0], b[0])`, not `a[0] - b[0]`: the subtraction overflows when values are near ±2³¹ and silently gives the wrong order.

```java
int[][] m = {{9, 10}, {13, 15}, {10, 12}};
Arrays.sort(m, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));
boolean ok = true;
for (int i = 0; i + 1 < m.length; i++)
    if (m[i][1] > m[i + 1][0]) ok = false;
System.out.println(ok);   // true
```
